! ipl_boot_big.strap -- the same IPL path as ipl_boot.strap, but with
! an image too big for the sixteen-word register tape.
!
! WHY A SECOND, LARGER PROGRAM.  ipl_boot.strap is four words
! including its control word, so it fits mkTapeSourceVector and proves
! the SEQUENCE.  It cannot prove the SIZE: a path that silently
! truncated at sixteen words would pass it.  This one assembles to
! more than sixteen words, so it can only load from a source that
! holds more than sixteen -- which is the whole point of
! mkTapeSourceFile (docs/boot_plan.md M0 step 1).
!
! AND THE SIZE IS NOT ARBITRARY.  MCP's IPL bootstrap is 369 words:
! printed p.2's control word reads count 0o560 = 368 plus the control
! word itself.  Sixteen is not within a factor of twenty of that, so
! until a tape can hold a real image, boot_plan.md M3 cannot be
! attempted at all.  This program is the smallest thing that
! distinguishes "the sequence works" from "the sequence works at
! scale".
!
! Same shape as ipl_boot.strap otherwise: first instruction BD because
! Reference Manual printed p.126 requires it, halt with B,$ rather
! than BEW,$ so the interruption system stays disabled as IPL left it.
!
! THE TAIL IS DATA, NOT PADDING-FOR-ITS-OWN-SAKE.  The last word is
! checked, so a load that dropped the end of the image -- exactly what
! a sixteen-word ceiling would do -- fails on MARKER rather than
! passing quietly.

        SLC, (8)100.0
IPLBS   BD,  GO
GO      LVI, $X1, 1234
        SX,  $X1, RESULT
        LVI, $X2, 4321
        SX,  $X2, RESULT2
HALT    B,   $
RESULT  DD(BU,64), 0
RESULT2 DD(BU,64), 0
! ---- ballast past the sixteen-word line -------------------------
        DD(BU,64), 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8
        DD(BU,64), 9,10,11,12
MARKER  DD(BU,64), (8)7777777
        END, IPLBS
